SU(2) generic full-cone transport counterexample audit

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_residual_transport_structure
./probe_su2_residual_transport_structure 8 4

OUTPUT
SU2_RESIDUAL_TRANSPORT_STRUCTURE maximum_level=16 maximum_coefficient=4 log_concave_full_profiles=1220 nonmonotone_reflected_ratios=12 sign_change_counts=[1208,0,12,0,0,0,0,0,0] transforms=7403 boundary_failures=18 first_nonmonotone=[2,3,4,0] first_nonmonotone_changes=2 first_failure_factor=1 first_failure_input=[2,3,4,4,1] first_failure_output=[9,23,29,24,9]

METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=8, the audit exhausts every nonzero integer
profile with coefficients in {0,1,2,3,4}.  It retains exactly the
profiles that are log concave with interval support and satisfy every
anchored current.  For every nontrivial irreducible factor Q, it applies
the exact finite fusion matrix N_Q twice and checks every output
simple-current boundary.

The first failure is the exact profile

  d=(2,3,4,4,1),
  N_1^2 d=(9,23,29,24,9).

Its radius-three output boundary current is

  9*23-24*9=-9.

Thus generic log-concave full-cone-to-boundary transport is false.
This does not test or disprove square-boundary insertion: the input is
an abstract profile and the square-root/orbit hypothesis is deliberately
absent.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror \
  probe_su2_residual_transport_structure.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_residual_transport_structure

